%I A401341 #19 Nov 19 2026 17:30:25
%S A401341 18,15,45,10,285,126,250,120,111,750,1998,1893,1665,1554,1443,1395,
%T A401341 2757,3885,2528,4995,1185,2331,2250,4250,1041,7245,7200,6055,16794,
%U A401341 16000,29250,14400,13986,27750,27218,13320,13206,12987,25974,12800,12500,37500,12321,12240,11655,46750
%N A401341 a(n) is the least term k in A401339 that produces the primitive quotient A401340(n).
%C A401341 Observation: a primitive quotient appears either to have a unique producer in A401339, or to have a finite number < 10 of such producers.
%C A401341 Case of unique producer: for example, the quotients 6, 7, 9, 73 and 81 have respectively only the producers 18, 15, 45, 285 and 128.
%C A401341 Case of a finite number (> 1 but < 10) of producers: let Z(k) the number obtained by inserting a 0 between each pair of consecutive digits of k. If k is a producer of a quotient q, we have Z(k)/k = q; if also the multiplication of k by r does not produce any carry, then Z(r*k) = r*Z(k) and Z(r*k)/(r*k) = q; consequently, r*k is also a producer of q. For example, the quotient 10 is obtained with nine producers (see example); while 85 is obtained with 120, 240, 360 and 480.
%e A401341 A401340(1) = 6 and 18 is the smallest (and unique) term in A401339 such that 108/18 = 6, so a(1) = 18.
%e A401341 A401340(4) = 10, and 100/10 = 200/20 = 300/30 = ... = 800/80 = 900/90 = 10; as 10 < 20 < 30 < ... < 80 < 90, then a(4) = 10.
%o A401341 (PARI) \\ where listap and f are defined in A401340
%o A401341 find(k) = my(m=1); while (f(m)/m != k, m++); m;
%o A401341 listak(nn) = my(v=listap(nn)); vector(#v, n, find(v[n]));
%Y A401341 Cf. A343550, A343551, A401339, A401340.
%K A401341 nonn,new
%O A401341 1,1
%A A401341 _Bernard Schott_, Nov 13 2026
%E A401341 More terms from _Michel Marcus_, Nov 14 2026
